## Tracks

Two lines of parallel work, matching the two actors from turn 2:

- **chair** — every AV check, slide load, changeover, talk, Q&A, opening/closing remark, and the final AV strike. One person, so these are strictly sequential even though they touch different resources (projector, lapel pack, lectern, AV desk).
- **catering** — coffee station setup, service to the foyer, foyer clearing. A different person/thread, running in parallel and only touching the chair's track at two points: it must wait for the second Q&A to end before sending coffee out, and the chair's changeover to the third speaker must wait for the foyer to be cleared.

## Triggers

Chair track chains `afterStep` end-to-end in the order given, since one person can only do one thing at a time. Catering track starts with `programStartOffset` (90 min in), then chains `afterStep`, except the coffee-to-foyer step and the third changeover, which each need to wait on a condition in the *other* track — handled with `compound`/`all`.

## Refining the question

Better question: *"Beyond who-hands-off-to-whom, what needs to already be warmed up, powered on, or in a stable state before the next action can safely begin — and where does one track's readiness gate the other even though the text doesn't spell out the wait?"* Two things fall out of this: (1) a projector fresh off cold power-on at the AV desk needs a short warm-up before its resolution/keystone/cue-slide test means anything — the text jumps straight from "line check" to "test the projector" as if the bulb is already stable, but that's an assumption, not a stated fact, so I've added an inferred `projector-warmup` step; (2) "coffee goes out... when the second talk's questions end" only works if the station is *already* built — the text never says the 90-minute setup must finish first, so `coffee-to-foyer` now waits on both conditions via `compound`/`all` instead of trusting the clock alone.

